sections.wikitext:
[[Paul McCartney]] began writing "My Love" on piano as a love song to his wife Linda.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=43}}{{sfn|Elson|1986|p=198}} He said he wrote it early on in their relationship; McCartney biographer Luca Perasi dates the composition to 1969 or 1970.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=86}} The song is a piano ballad in the style of McCartney's [[Beatles]] song "[[The Long and Winding Road]]".{{sfn|Woffinden|1981|p=67}}{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}}

After forming the band [[Wings (band)|Wings]] with Linda in the summer of 1971,{{sfn|Snow|2013|pp=P-16, P-22}} McCartney included "My Love" in the set lists for the group's two concert tours in 1972.{{sfn|Badman|2001|pp=64, 76}}{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|pp=167, 169–71}} When they performed it at [[University of Nottingham|Nottingham University]] on 9 February for Wings' public debut, the song included Linda singing lines in response to McCartney's lead vocal. According to Perasi, the performance was otherwise "almost identical" to the version that the band subsequently recorded for official release.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|pp=86–87}}

The song is in the [[Key (music)|key]] of [[F major]].{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} It opens with an extended ambient note before the vocal enters.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}} The composition has an AABAA structure followed by an outro, with the A sections comprising verse-choruses and the B section containing a bridge.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} According to musicologist Vincent Benitez, the verses establish a "sense of instability" through lyrics such as "And when I go away" and "And when the cupboard's bare", implying distance and material emptiness, respectively, and this mood is supported by the inclusion of chords such as B{{music|flat}}maj7 and D9 that suggest a departure from the home key. He says the choruses and the bridge then convey the emotional security provided by the singer's lover – lyrically, and through the incorporation of a plagal progression (in the chorus) and other chords that correspond with F major.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} The outro consists of the first part of the bridge;{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} McCartney sings "Only my love does it good" before pausing and then returning with a prolonged vocalisation of the word "to" then resolving to "me".{{sfn|Sheffield|2017|pp=209–10}}

