Paul McCartney began writing "My Love" on piano as a love song to his wife Linda.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=43}}{{sfn|Elson|1986|p=198}} He said he wrote it early on in their relationship; McCartney biographer Luca Perasi dates the composition to 1969 or 1970.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=86}} The song is a piano ballad in the style of McCartney's Beatles song "The Long and Winding Road".{{sfn|Woffinden|1981|p=67}}{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}}

After forming the band Wings with Linda in the summer of 1971,{{sfn|Snow|2013|pp=P-16, P-22}} McCartney included "My Love" in the set lists for the group's two concert tours in 1972.{{sfn|Badman|2001|pp=64, 76}}{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|pp=167, 169–71}} When they performed it at Nottingham University on 9 February for Wings' public debut, the song included Linda singing lines in response to McCartney's lead vocal. According to Perasi, the performance was otherwise "almost identical" to the version that the band subsequently recorded for official release.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|pp=86–87}}

The song is in the key of F major.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} It opens with an extended ambient note before the vocal enters.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}} The composition has an AABAA structure followed by an outro, with the A sections comprising verse-choruses and the B section containing a bridge.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} According to musicologist Vincent Benitez, the verses establish a "sense of instability" through lyrics such as "And when I go away" and "And when the cupboard's bare", implying distance and material emptiness, respectively, and this mood is supported by the inclusion of chords such as B{{music|flat}}maj7 and D9 that suggest a departure from the home key. He says the choruses and the bridge then convey the emotional security provided by the singer's lover – lyrically, and through the incorporation of a plagal progression (in the chorus) and other chords that correspond with F major.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} The outro consists of the first part of the bridge;{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=44}} McCartney sings "Only my love does it good" before pausing and then returning with a prolonged vocalisation of the word "to" then resolving to "me".{{sfn|Sheffield|2017|pp=209–10}}

McCartney invited Richard Hewson, with whom he had worked before while with the Beatles, to arrange the orchestral accompaniment for "My Love". The song was recorded live at Abbey Road Studios in London{{sfn|Doyle|2013|pp=82–83}} with a 50-piece orchestra accompanying the band. The session took place in January 1973, late in the recording for Wings' second album, Red Rose Speedway.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|pp=85, 86}} McCartney played Fender Rhodes electric piano on the track,{{sfn|Elson|1986|p=122}} while Denny Laine substituted for McCartney on bass guitar. The idea to tape the basic track and the orchestral arrangement simultaneously went against music industry convention, since the session musicians were paid by the hour.{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=257}} Hewson recalled that he recruited "the best jazz musicians I knew ... They had this particular warm sound" and that the reason for the live recording was because McCartney wanted to capture "a certain feeling".{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}} In music journalist Tom Breihan's description, although the song appears to lack a formal structure, "It chugs and twinkles with the slow confidence of an old torch song, while the orchestra ... swells and contracts."

<blockquote>I'd sort of written the solo, as I often did write our solos. And he walked up to me right before the take and said, "Hey, would it be alright if I try something else?" And I said, "Er ... yeah." It was like, "Do I believe in this guy?" And he played the solo on "My Love", which came right out of the blue. And I just thought, Fucking great. And so there were plenty of moments like that where somebody's skill or feeling would overtake my wishes.</blockquote>

According to McCullough, it was the first time anyone in Wings had challenged McCartney, and it was an approach that others in the band encouraged, in an effort to make Wings a genuine band and improve McCartney's image.{{sfn|McGee|2003|pp=161–62}} He described the result on "My Love" as "a stroke of luck, a gift from God really, and you get that in music".{{sfn|Doyle|2013|p=83}}{{refn|group=nb|In 2010, McCullough told The Irish News that he had drawn inspiration from "the power of the orchestra, the electric guitar slung round my neck and knowing that if I didn't come up with something I'd have to slink away with my head bowed".}} In his book The Lyrics: 1956 to the Present (2021), McCartney called the solo "absolutely beautiful" and was proud he gave McCullough the freedom to improvise given his short tenure in the band at that point.{{sfn|McCartney|2021|pp=499–500}}

sections.wikitext:
[[Apple Records]] issued "My Love" as a single on 23 March 1973, with the US release following on 9 April.{{sfn|Badman|2001|p=93}}{{sfn|Castleman|Podrazik|1976|p=122}} The B-side was "The Mess",{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=48}} recorded live at the [[World Forum (The Hague)|Netherlands Congresgebouw]] in The Hague on 21 August 1972.{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|p=173}}{{sfn|Badman|2001|p=78}}{{refn|group=nb|Although essentially a live recording, "The Mess" was subjected to heavy editing and some overdubbing in the studio.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=84}} Portions of up to 45 seconds were excised to reduce the song's length and the vocals were heavily processed.{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|p=173}}}} For the first time in the group's career, the release was credited to "Paul McCartney & Wings", rather than Wings alone.{{sfn|Spizer|2005|p=153}} The name change was made in the belief that the disappointing sales of Wings' 1971 debut, ''[[Wild Life (Wings album)|Wild Life]]'', were due to the public being unaware of McCartney's involvement.{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=258}}{{sfn|Schaffner|1978|p=157}} ''Red Rose Speedway'' was released on 30 April 1973{{sfn|Spizer|2005|p=155}} and was similarly credited to Paul McCartney & Wings.{{sfn|Woffinden|1981|pp=66–67}}{{sfn|McGee|2003|p=40}} "My Love" was sequenced as the album's second track, between "[[Big Barn Bed]]" and "Get on the Right Thing".{{sfn|Carr|Tyler|1978|p=105}}

The single's release marked the start of a highly active period for Wings.{{sfn|Woffinden|1981|p=66}}{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=137}} The band filmed a promotional clip for the song, which used an alternate McCartney lead vocal.{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|p=176}} They also promoted the single on the ''[[James Paul McCartney (TV program)|James Paul McCartney]]'' TV special.{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}} McCartney had agreed to do the special in return for [[Lew Grade]], whose company [[Associated Television|ATV]] owned the [[Northern Songs]] publishing catalogue,{{sfn|Woffinden|1981|p=67}} dropping his legal objections to Linda being credited as a co-writer on many of his songs since 1971.{{sfn|Schaffner|1978|pp=151, 156}}{{sfn|Sounes|2010|pp=303–04}} The band filmed a performance of "My Love" for ''[[Top of the Pops]]'', which was shown on the 4 and 11 April editions of the show.{{sfn|Badman|2001|p=95}} Immediately after completing this performance, McCullough vomited on the stage;{{sfn|McGee|2003|pp=46–47}} drunk beforehand,{{sfn|Doyle|2013|p=83}}{{sfn|Norman|2016|pp=468–69}} he had become nauseated by the studio smoke effects.{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=208}} The incident had an adverse effect on his already fractious relationship with McCartney.{{sfn|Doyle|2013|p=83}}{{sfn|McGee|2003|pp=46–47}} Wings played "My Love" throughout their [[Wings 1973 UK Tour|1973 UK tour]].{{sfn|McGee|2003|pp=173–74}} These live performances were the source of frustration for McCullough, who was denied the freedom to improvise when playing the solo.{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=258}} Adhering to a populist approach over McCullough's [[blues]] sensibilities, McCartney insisted that he reproduce the solo exactly as heard on the studio recording.{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|p=176}}{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|pp=208, 258}}

The single topped the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] for four weeks{{sfn|Spizer|2005|p=153}} and peaked at number nine on the [[UK Singles Chart]].{{sfn|Schaffner|1978|p=167}} The song also went to number one on ''Billboard''{{'}}s [[Hot Adult Contemporary|Easy Listening]] chart for three weeks.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.billboard.com/artist/paul-mccartney/chart-history/asi/|title=Paul McCartney Chart History: Adult Contemporary > My Love – Paul McCartney/Wings|publisher=[[Billboard (magazine)|billboard.com]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171107092810/https://www.billboard.com/music/paul-mccartney/chart-history/adult-contemporary|archive-date=7 November 2017|access-date=21 July 2019}}</ref> On the ''Billboard'' Hot 100, it was demoted by [[George Harrison]]'s "[[Give Me Love (Give Me Peace on Earth)]]" in late June,{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=258}}{{sfn|Clayson|2003|p=162}} marking the first time since 25 April 1964 that the Beatles had occupied the top two positions on the chart,{{sfn|Castleman|Podrazik|1976|pp=347–53}} and the only occasion that any of its former members have done so as solo artists.{{sfn|Snow|2013|p=G-39}} The popularity of "My Love" also contributed to the commercial success of ''Red Rose Speedway'',{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=137}} which became the first of five consecutive Wings albums to top the ''Billboard'' LPs chart;{{sfn|McGee|2003|pp=39, 245}} according to author [[Bruce Spizer]], it was "the song that sold the album".{{sfn|Spizer|2005|p=156}} The single was certified [[Recording Industry Association of America certification|Gold]] by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]] on 9 July,{{sfn|Castleman|Podrazik|1976|p=332}} for sales of over one million copies.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=paul+mc+cartney&ti=my+love&lab=&genre=&format=&date_option=release&from=&to=&award=&type=&category=&adv=SEARCH#search_section |title=Search: Paul McCartney + My Love |access-date=20 April 2018 |publisher=[[RIAA]] |archive-date=25 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125055923/https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=paul+mc+cartney&ti=my+love&lab=&genre=&format=&date_option=release&from=&to=&award=&type=&category=&adv=SEARCH#search_section |url-status=live }}</ref> ''Billboard'' ranked "My Love" at number five on its [[Billboard Year-End Hot 100 singles of 1973|Year-End Hot 100 singles of 1973]] chart.<ref name="BYE" />

The song was subsequently included on the 1978 Wings compilation album ''[[Wings Greatest]]''.{{sfn|McGee|2003|p=190}} It has also appeared on the McCartney compilations ''[[All the Best!]]'' (1987), ''[[Wingspan: Hits and History]]'' (2001),{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}} ''[[Pure McCartney (Paul McCartney album)|Pure McCartney]]'' (2016),<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.popmatters.com/paul-mccartney-pure-mccartney-2495428479.html|first=Chris|last=Gerard|title=Paul McCartney: Pure McCartney|website=[[PopMatters]]|date=13 June 2016|access-date=17 July 2019|archive-date=17 July 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190717085446/https://www.popmatters.com/paul-mccartney-pure-mccartney-2495428479.html|url-status=live}}</ref> ''[[Wings (Paul McCartney and Wings album)|Wings]]'' (2025)<ref>{{Cite web |last=Skinner |first=Tom |date=9 September 2025 |title=Wings announce self-titled anthology collection |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/wings-announce-self-titled-anthology-collection-3891215 |access-date=9 September 2025 |website=[[NME]] |language=en-GB |archive-date=9 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250909231017/https://www.nme.com/news/music/wings-announce-self-titled-anthology-collection-3891215 |url-status=live }}</ref> and on the 2022 box set ''[[The 7" Singles Box]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.paulmccartney.com/news/paul-announces-the-7-singles-box|title='The 7" Singles Box' – Out 2 December 2022|website=PaulMcCartney.com|date=10 November 2022|access-date=5 December 2022}}</ref> A live version from Wings' [[Wings Over the World tour#Wings over America|1976 US tour]], with McCullough's replacement, [[Jimmy McCulloch]], on lead guitar, was released on the ''[[Wings over America]]'' triple album.{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|pp=174, 213–14}} McCartney has since included live performances of the song on his albums ''[[Paul Is Live]]'' (1993), ''[[Back in the World]]'' (2002) and ''[[Good Evening New York City]]'' (2009).{{sfn|Perasi|2013|p=87}}

