Rodriguez describes "My Love" as McCartney's "first post-Beatles evergreen" and a [[Standard (music)|standard]], due to its instant popularity among other recording artists.{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=183}} In 1976, Linda reflected that ''Red Rose Speedway'' was "such a non-confident record" made during a "terribly unsure period", yet it still contained "beautiful songs" such as "My Love".{{sfn|McGee|2003|p=39}}<ref>{{cite magazine|first=Barbara|last=Charone|title=Linda McCartney: Silly Love Songs|magazine=[[Sounds (magazine)|Sounds]]|date=3 April 1976}} Available at [http://www.rocksbackpages.com/Library/Article/linda-mccartney-silly-love-songs Rock's Backpages] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190713160414/https://www.rocksbackpages.com/Library/Article/linda-mccartney-silly-love-songs |date=13 July 2019 }} (subscription required).</ref> In 1986, McCartney selected it as his favourite track from the Wings era and recalled the time as "a romantic period, folks!"{{sfn|Perasi|2013|pp=87–88}} McCullough, whose post-Wings career included playing with [[Joe Cocker]] and [[Donovan]], and recording albums under his own name,{{sfn|Rodriguez|2010|p=210}} considered the guitar solo on "My Love" to be the best of his career.{{sfn|Spizer|2005|p=156}}

Following Linda's death in 1998, "My Love" was among the songs McCartney chose for the musical program at the two memorial services held in her memory, in London and New York City.{{sfn|Badman|2001|p=594}} McCartney was highly public in his expression of grief{{sfn|Doggett|2011|p=335}} and organised the services with what biographer [[Howard Sounes]] describes as "the same professionalism he brought to his concert performances".{{sfn|Sounes|2010|p=482}} Arranged for strings, the song was the closing piece{{sfn|Badman|2001|p=594}} performed by the [[Brodsky Quartet]]{{sfn|Clayson|2003|p=255}} at [[St Martin-in-the-Fields]] church in London's [[Trafalgar Square]] on 8 June.{{sfn|Sounes|2010|p=482}} At the New York service, held at [[Riverside Church]] in [[Upper Manhattan]] on 22 June,{{sfn|Badman|2001|p=596}} it was performed by the Loma Mar Quartet.{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|p=410}} Both events were given heavy coverage by news media.{{sfn|Badman|2001|pp=594, 596}} McCartney also included the song among the Linda-inspired compositions recorded for his 1999 [[classical music]] album ''[[Working Classical]]'',{{sfn|Sounes|2010|p=496}} where it was again performed by the Loma Mar Quartet.{{sfn|Madinger|Easter|2000|pp=409–10}}

McCartney included "My Love" in the set list for his [[Driving World Tour]] in 2002,<ref name="EW">{{cite magazine|author=EW staff|title=Paul McCartney: File Under ... Concerts|url=https://ew.com/article/2002/05/10/paul-mccartney-file-underconcerts/|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|date=10 May 2002|access-date=19 July 2019|archive-date=19 July 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190719061208/https://ew.com/article/2002/05/10/paul-mccartney-file-underconcerts/|url-status=live}}</ref> when, bolstered by the support of his second wife, [[Heather Mills]], he returned to touring for the first time since 1993.{{sfn|Sounes|2010|pp=510–12}} McCartney performed the song in Linda's memory, as his renditions of "Something" and "[[Here Today (Paul McCartney song)|Here Today]]" were tributes to his former Beatles bandmates Harrison and [[John Lennon]], respectively.<ref name="EW" />{{sfn|Sounes|2010|pp=512–13}}{{refn|group=nb|McCartney's 2001 album ''[[Driving Rain]]'' included "About You", in which he expressed his gratitude to Mills for her help when grieving Linda's death.{{sfn|Benitez|2010|p=158}} Norman describes the album's central theme as "how Heather had rescued him from tempests of grief".{{sfn|Norman|2016|p=723}}}} Following his acrimonious divorce from Mills in 2008, he again performed the song in concert as a tribute to Linda. According to Sounes, whereas the live renditions of "My Love" had "seemingly irritated" Mills on the 2002 tour, McCartney's performances in 2008 were accompanied by pictures of Linda projected onto "huge screens" with "all images of Heather excised".{{sfn|Sounes|2010|pp=512, 558}}

